  "question": {
    "text": "An amplitude-modulated wave is represented by, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>cos</mi><mn>12560</mn><mi>t</mi><mo>)</mo><mi>sin</mi><mfenced><mrow><mn>111</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>4</mn></msup><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfenced><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi></math>. The modulating frequency in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>kHz</mi></math> will be"
  },
  "options": [],
  "correct_answer": "2",
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>The function of the amplitude-modulated wave is represented by,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi></msub><mfenced close=\"]\" open=\"[\" separators=\"|\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>A</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></msub><msub><mi>A</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi></msub></mfrac><mi>sin</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#960;</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></msub><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfenced></math>, now compare it from the given equation,&nbsp;<br /><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>cos</mi><mn>12560</mn><mi>t</mi><mo>)</mo><mi>sin</mi><mfenced><mrow><mn>111</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>4</mn></msup><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfenced><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8658;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#960;</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>12560</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8658;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2000</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>Hz</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>kHz</mi></math>.</p>"
